---
// The page for a company that wants an agent like Benny built for its own team, at `/for-teams`:
// what they get, what gets set up, how the work goes, and the inquiry form. It shares the main
// page's look and example thread but none of its sentences, so search engines treat it as its own
// page. It makes no claims about customers or prices, because there are none to state yet.
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import ExampleThread from "../components/ExampleThread.astro";
import InquiryForm from "../components/InquiryForm.astro";
import ClosingPanel from "../components/ClosingPanel.astro";
import "../styles/landing.css";

const repo = "https://github.com/madscientist111/benny";
---

<Page
	title="A self-hosted AI software engineer for your team's GitHub | Benny"
	description="I set up an AI software engineer like Benny for your team: it works from your GitHub issues, runs on your own Cloudflare account and uses the AI subscriptions you already pay for. A self-hosted alternative to Devin."
>
<div class="band-blue"><div class="in">
  <div class="nav">
    <a class="mark" href="/">Benny <span class="mark-tag">for teams</span></a>
    <ul><li><a href="#get">What you get</a></li><li><a href="#setup">What I set up</a></li><li><a href="#steps">How it works</a></li><li><a href="/">Self-host Benny</a></li></ul>
    <a class="gh" href="#ask">Get in touch</a>
  </div>
  <div class="hero">
    <p class="oss"><svg><use href="#i-star"/></svg>Built on Benny, which is open source</p>
    <h1 class="display"><span>An AI engineer</span> <span>inside <img class="sticker" src="/art/team-hero.webp" alt="Benny the beaver, waving, with a staff badge round his neck" /> your team's</span> <span>GitHub</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">I set one up for your team. It takes work from your issues, runs on your own cloud and uses the AI subscriptions you already pay for. Nothing passes through a vendor.</p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn" href="#ask">Let's talk</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href={repo}><svg><use href="#i-gh"/></svg>Read the code first</a>
    </div>
    <ExampleThread forTeam />
  </div>
</div></div>

<section class="facts-sec" id="get"><div class="in">
  <div class="facts">
    <div><div class="swap" tabindex="0"><img class="sticker one" src="/art/team-lodge.webp" alt="Benny at home in his lodge, a padlock on the door" /><img class="sticker two" src="/art/team-lodge2.webp" alt="" aria-hidden="true" /></div><h3>Your code stays with you</h3><p>The agent runs inside your own cloud account. Your code and your prompts go to no one else, and you can read every line of what runs.</p></div>
    <div><div class="swap" tabindex="0"><img class="sticker one" src="/art/team-bump.webp" alt="Benny fist-bumping a teammate" /><img class="sticker two" src="/art/team-bump2.webp" alt="" aria-hidden="true" /></div><h3>No new tool for your team</h3><p>Engineers ask in an issue, the way they would ask a colleague, and review a pull request. There is no dashboard to roll out and nothing to train people on.</p></div>
    <div><div class="swap" tabindex="0"><img class="sticker one" src="/art/team-stamp.webp" alt="Benny holding a pull request up for a person to approve" /><img class="sticker two" src="/art/team-stamp2.webp" alt="" aria-hidden="true" /></div><h3>A person always merges</h3><p>The agent opens pull requests and has no way to merge one. That limit is in the code, not in a prompt.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<section class="cf setup" id="setup"><div class="in grid">
  <div class="side">
    <h2 class="sec-title">What I set up for you</h2>
    <p>Benny is where I start. What your team needs decides what I change.</p>
    <div class="swap setup-art" tabindex="0"><img class="sticker one" src="/art/laptop.webp" alt="Benny typing on a laptop" /><img class="sticker two" src="/art/laptop2.webp" alt="" aria-hidden="true" /></div>
      </div>
  <div>
    <dl>
      <div><dt>The agent</dt><dd>Deployed on your Cloudflare account: one agent per issue, asleep and free between comments.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Your GitHub App</dt><dd>Under your organisation's name, with only the permissions it needs and none over your workflows.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Your rules</dt><dd>Who may give it work, which branches it may push to, and when it must stop and ask.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Schedules</dt><dd>Repeating work, such as a weekday digest of what merged and what is stuck.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Your models</dt><dd>It runs on Claude Code or OpenCode Go today, on the subscription you already have.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</div></section>

<section id="steps"><div class="in steps">
  <h2 class="sec-title">How an engagement works</h2>
  <div class="plain-steps">
    <div class="step-line"><h3><i>1</i>Introduction</h3><p>You send a short brief. We then go through your repositories, your review process and the work you want the agent to take on.</p></div>
    <div class="step-line"><h3><i>2</i>Scope and permissions</h3><p>Before any work starts you get a written scope: what the agent can read and change, who can assign it work, and what always needs a person's approval.</p></div>
    <div class="step-line"><h3><i>3</i>Deployment on your accounts</h3><p>I deploy the agent to your Cloudflare account and register the GitHub App under your organisation. You own the accounts, the keys and the code from the first day.</p></div>
    <div class="step-line"><h3><i>4</i>Handover</h3><p>Your team assigns work by mentioning the agent in an issue. I stay on through the first pull requests and adjust it to how your team reviews.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<InquiryForm />

<ClosingPanel
	heading="Not sure yet? Read the code. It is all open."
	text="Benny is open source under the MIT licence. Your team can run it without me; I am here if you want it shaped to how you work."
	forTeam
/>
</Page>
